<LessonFrame Number="7">
    <Try>
        <ol class="steps">
            <li>Open <b>Radar</b>, <b>Comms</b> and <b>Power</b>. Their addresses share a prefix, but the console around them is one layout,
                and the frame labelled <code>ConsoleLayout</code> never changes.</li>
            <li>Watch the right-hand panel. Radar and Comms each fill it with their own inspector. Power declares none, so the panel shows
                its empty state.</li>
            <li>Look at the chain above. The console is a level of its own, but it adds nothing to the address.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="dim">A group shares a layout (or a guard, or a loader) between routes without adding a segment to their URLs. A named
            outlet is a second place a route can render into.</p>
    </Try>
    <Stage>
        <XRay Name="Lesson7Page" Level="0">
            <ModRouterOutlet>
                <EmptyContent><p class="dim">Open a console page.</p></EmptyContent>
            </ModRouterOutlet>
        </XRay>
    </Stage>
    <Code>
        <CodeRef File="LabRoutes.cs"><code>.Group(layout, ...)</code> and <code>.Slot("side", ...)</code></CodeRef>
        <CodeRef File="Lessons/L7/ConsoleLayout.razor">the group's layout: the page outlet and a named outlet</CodeRef>
        <CodeRef File="Lessons/L7/RadarInspector.razor">a component that fills the named outlet</CodeRef>
    </Code>
</LessonFrame>
